  <!-- OSINT METHODOLOGY -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">OSINT Investigation Methodology</div>
    <div class="method-steps">
      <div class="method-step"><span class="method-num">01</span><div class="method-text"><strong>Define the target.</strong> Establish what you're looking for — IP range, domain, threat actor alias, or email address. Set clear scope to avoid scope creep.</div></div>
      <div class="method-step"><span class="method-num">02</span><div class="method-text"><strong>Passive recon first.</strong> Start with read-only sources — WHOIS, passive DNS, Shodan, Censys. Never touch the target directly at this stage.</div></div>
      <div class="method-step"><span class="method-num">03</span><div class="method-text"><strong>Pivot on indicators.</strong> Each IOC is a pivot point. A domain leads to an IP → ASN → registrar pattern → other domains. Follow the thread.</div></div>
      <div class="method-step"><span class="method-num">04</span><div class="method-text"><strong>Cross-reference sources.</strong> Validate findings across at least two independent sources. Single-source IOCs are unreliable for attribution.</div></div>
      <div class="method-step"><span class="method-num">05</span><div class="method-text"><strong>Map to ATT&CK.</strong> Translate infrastructure and behavioural findings to MITRE ATT&CK techniques to build a structured threat profile.</div></div>
      <div class="method-step"><span class="method-num">06</span><div class="method-text"><strong>Document and share responsibly.</strong> Record all findings with timestamps and source citations. Share IOCs via structured formats (STIX/TAXII) where appropriate.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

